"Formosa means beautiful in Portuguese. A story taught in Taiwan’s textbooks says that Portuguese sailors in the 1500s saw the island and called it Ilha Formosa, “beautiful island.” In 2017, the historian Weng Jiayin and the writer Huang Yan argued that the island those sailors called Formosa was probably Okinawa, and that the Spanish were the first to give the name to Taiwan, in the 1580s. In 2020, Paul Kua answered that Portuguese maps already named Taiwan this way in the 1550s.": "11adbb948397",
"Imagine drawing a map of a place you have only seen from a ship. You can see the coast, but not the mountains behind it, and not the far side. The maps in this lesson were made by outsiders in Europe, and they show the island as those mapmakers knew it. All that time, Indigenous peoples were living across the island, in the mountains, on the plains, and along both coasts. They are not shown on these maps, but they were there.": "98482b321232",
"Imagine drawing a map of a place you have only seen from a ship. You can see the coast, but not the mountains behind it, and not the far side. The maps in this lesson were made by outsiders in Europe, and they show the island as those mapmakers knew it. All that time, Indigenous peoples were living across the island, in the mountains, on the plains, and along both coasts. They are not shown on these maps, but they were there. The oldest map in this lesson is dated 1584. Ludovico Georgio, a Portuguese mapmaker, first drew it in 1579, and it was printed in an atlas by Abraham Ortelius. West is at the top. Off the coast of China are two islands labeled “Lequeio parua” and “Ins. Fermosa.” Ins. is Latin for island, and Fermosa is an old spelling of Formosa. The historian Paul Kua reads the two together as Taiwan, drawn in two pieces. Formosa means beautiful in Portuguese. A story taught in Taiwan’s textbooks says that Portuguese sailors in the 1500s saw the island and called it Ilha Formosa, “beautiful island.” In 2017, the historian Weng Jiayin and the writer Huang Yan argued that the island those sailors called Formosa was probably Okinawa, and that the Spanish were the first to give the name to Taiwan, in the 1580s. In 2020, Paul Kua answered that Portuguese maps already named Taiwan this way in the 1550s. In 1728, a sea chart printed in London still drew Taiwan long and much too thin. Nine years later, in 1737, a French geographer named d’Anville published a map that looks much closer to today’s. Its title says it was drawn from maps that the Kangxi Emperor had Jesuit missionaries make on the spot. On that map, the west coast is full of place names, but the east coast is almost empty. In 1764, Jacques Nicolas Bellin, a mapmaker for the French navy, wrote something honest right on his map. Across the mountains: “the inside of this island is not known.” Along the east coast: “this whole coast is very little known.” The mountains and the east coast were not empty, of course. Indigenous peoples lived there. Those places were unknown to the mapmaker, not empty. Where does the name Taiwan come from? Victor Mair, a scholar of Chinese languages, writes that it comes from the name of an Indigenous people in the southwest of the island. A Dutch ship’s log from 1622 already spelled the area “Teijoan” and “Taiyowan.” On the maps in this lesson, the name is used in two ways, sometimes on the same map: for the whole island (“Tai-ouan ou Isle Formose”) and for one place on the west coast (“Tai-ouan ou Taiwan,” beside a ruined Dutch fort).": "7eb69e086873",
"In 1728, a sea chart printed in London still drew Taiwan long and much too thin. Nine years later, in 1737, a French geographer named d’Anville published a map that looks much closer to today’s. Its title says it was drawn from maps that the Kangxi Emperor had Jesuit missionaries make on the spot. On that map, the west coast is full of place names, but the east coast is almost empty.": "9736fa7dc95e",
"In 1764, Jacques Nicolas Bellin, a mapmaker for the French navy, wrote something honest right on his map. Across the mountains: “the inside of this island is not known.” Along the east coast: “this whole coast is very little known.” The mountains and the east coast were not empty, of course. Indigenous peoples lived there. Those places were unknown to the mapmaker, not empty.": "fd685f8816ca",
"Its title says the map was based on surveys made for the Kangxi Emperor.": "76cff98963da",
